{\b Installing}\
Drag PsyMP3 onto the Applications folder beside it.\
\
{\b Opening PsyMP3 the first time}\
PsyMP3 is not signed with an Apple Developer ID and is not notarized by Apple. Because of that, the first time you open it, macOS stops it with a message that it cannot check PsyMP3 for malicious software, or that Apple could not verify it. Nothing is wrong with your copy. To open it:\
\pard\li360\fi-360\sa120\partightenfactor0
1.\tab Open PsyMP3 from Applications. When macOS refuses, click {\b Done} (or {\b OK}).\
2.\tab Open {\b System Settings} and go to {\b Privacy & Security}.\
3.\tab Scroll down to {\b Security}. Next to the line about PsyMP3 being blocked, click {\b Open Anyway}, and enter your password or use Touch ID.\
4.\tab In the box that follows, click {\b Open Anyway} again.\
\pard\sa200\partightenfactor0
macOS asks only this once. After that, PsyMP3 opens like any other app.\
\
On macOS 14 Sonoma there is a shortcut: Control-click PsyMP3 in Applications, choose {\b Open}, then click {\b Open} in the box that follows.\
\
{\b From Terminal instead}\
If you prefer, this command clears the flag macOS puts on downloaded files, and PsyMP3 then opens without asking:\
{\f1\fs24 xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/PsyMP3.app}\
